## A — INSTANT UNEDITED EXPORT AT MAX, WITH NOTHING REMOVED [medium · capture + compose]
WHAT ROBERT ASKED FOR: "i want video rendered while recording so non edit export instantly as we
supposed to have now but dont fucking work".

WHY IT DOESN'T WORK NOW, and it is one deliberate line, not a mystery: below max the app already
encodes the finished picture during the take and the INSTANT path packet-copies it — that is why an
unedited export is instant at every step except max. At max the composite is never opened, and
session.ts:1231 says why in its own comment: three encoders wanted 555 Mpx/s against the ~416 this
machine measures; without the composite it is 411, which fits. So max+camera has no fast path at all.

THE PROPOSAL — record THREE things at max instead of two, and lose nothing:
  1. THE FINISHED PICTURE — screen with the camera drawn on it, the stage exactly as recorded.
  2. THE PATCH — the small rectangle of clean screen hidden BEHIND the camera.
  3. THE CAMERA at 720p, exactly as today.
(1) + (2) ARE THE CLEAN SCREEN, pixel for pixel: the finished picture holds clean screen everywhere
except under the camera, the patch holds what is under it, both first generation off the same source
frames. So every edit that works today still works — move the camera anywhere, resize it up or down,
hide it, zoom, change the frame, change the step — because the clean screen is still there, stored in
two pieces instead of one. NOTHING IS REMOVED. What is ADDED is that the take now contains the
finished video, so an unedited max+camera export is a packet copy.

THE COST, and it is the whole argument:
  today at max      screen 356 + camera 55                    = 411 Mpx/s, 2 encoders
  proposed          finished picture 356 + patch ~21 + cam 55 = 431 Mpx/s, 3 encoders   (+5 %)
+5 % against a ceiling of ~416 that has never been measured at max/60 — and B14 says max is currently
recording at 30 fps, i.e. ~205 Mpx/s, so half the budget may be sitting unused behind a bug. B14 LANDS
FIRST AND ITS NUMBER DECIDES THIS. If the 5 % does not fit, this proposal dies there; O15's encoder
budget already drops the composite first, so the failure is graceful and not a lost take.
OPEN, MUST BE ANSWERED BEFORE ANY CODE: at max the finished picture becomes load-bearing, and today the
composite watchdog DROPS the mix after 5 s under 12 fps (liveCompositeV2.ts:366) — safe now because max
uses no composite, fatal if it is the take. Its failure has to open a raw-screen segment
(segmentContain's shape), never drop. Also: the composite runs `latencyMode:'realtime'` and the raw
path runs `'quality'`; if the composite becomes the shipped file that has to change and be re-measured.
WHAT IT DOES NOT BUY: an edited export. A camera move over the whole take, a frame preset, a step
change — all still render. This buys the unedited and cut cases at max, nothing more.

## B — "SAME AS LAST FRAME" FRAMES [light-medium · compose · nothing a user sees changes]
THE FACT, already in DECISIONS 2026-08-25 (4) and never acted on: capture is frame-driven and stores
~1 fps while the screen is still, and the render then re-inflates that to constant rate and encodes
30-60 identical pictures per second of a motionless screen. It was written down as "the order-of-
magnitude lever on screen content" and parked as X10=O5e because the fix on the table made the file
variable-rate, and Robert said "fuck this".
THE DIFFERENT FIX: every video format already has a picture that means "identical to the previous
one" — all blocks skipped, no residual. It costs ~10 bytes, needs no encoder, no decoder and no draw,
every player on earth plays it, and THE FILE STAYS CONSTANT RATE. Same duration, same timestamps, same
pixels. So when an output frame is identical to the one before it, write that instead of making a new
one. We already know which frames those are without touching a pixel: capture told us when it stored
one frame instead of sixty.
NOT A FORMAT AND NOT AN ENCODER: one all-skip P-slice against the stream's own SPS/PPS. Days of work,
and the gate is exact — the file must decode to the same pixels as today, frame for frame.
WHAT IT DOES NOT BUY, said plainly so nobody oversells it: WITH THE CAMERA ON THE PICTURE IS NEVER
STILL, so this does nothing for a camera take. It is a large win for screen-only exports and zero for
Robert's own headline case.
MEASURE FIRST, one number: what fraction of a real ≥60-min take is byte-identical frame to frame. The
detector exists (X17's census). That number decides whether the days are worth spending.

## C — ADDITIVE UI, NEVER A CONDITION OF SPEED [light]
Offered ONLY as things Robert may want anyway, never as the price of a fix (robert (23)(b) forbids
that framing): the frame preset chosen on the CAPTURE screen as well as in the editor, and the camera
pose set before the record press as well as dragged during the take (UI1 already allows the live drag).
Both are recorded into the take, so a take set up that way exports through the fast path instead of the
render. If either is refused, nothing else on this page changes.

## D — CHECKED AND DEAD. Do not re-derive; each cost a session or a measurement.
- OVERLAPPING DECODE AND ENCODE IS NOT A LEVER. R1's row still names it at ~1.4×. O5b says the export
  is already pipelined, and X4 measured the decode farm at 0.99-1.08× against a ≥2× gate — because
  decode and encode CONTEND for one media engine: the same decode takes 4.7 s alone and 17-23 s while
  the encoder runs beside it. A second JS thread does not create a second engine. R1's remaining lever
  should be re-read against that before anyone spends a session on it.
- A BACKGROUND FRAME SCALES THE SCREEN, so every pixel lands somewhere new and NO reuse scheme of any
  kind — chunks, block copies, hidden layers — can make it cheaper than a full pass. This is arithmetic.
  It is also why Robert killed the block-rewriter family (DECISIONS robert (23)(e)); see the Phase 5
  parking note in TASKS for what was learned before it died.
- Everything J2 researched and ruled out is listed in that same Phase 5 note: H.264 block patching,
  H.264 hidden pictures, a second full composite encoder at max, the 64 px and 4 px camera grids.

## E — THE ONE MEASUREMENT EVERYTHING ABOVE RESTS ON
Nobody has measured this machine's sustained encode at max and 60 fps. rate.ts says 416 Mpx/s;
scripts/encode-cost.mjs's header says 120-188 through a real render; Robert's own 124.8-min take ran at
309. They disagree 3.5×, and B14 says max is not even reaching 60 today. Until that number exists,
every encoder-budget argument on the roadmap — including proposal A's +5 % — is an argument about
numbers nobody has. B14 produces it as a side effect. Do B14.
